# Churn replication on new markets: plan (draft 3 with the review r3 caveats, 2026-11-16; consensus; no spend)

**Why.** Two external reviews of draft 10 (`docs/revision/roster/astra-pro-paper-d10-ext.md`, `-ext2.md`; WEIS 3/5 upper
borderline) found the churn result reproducible but narrow: one market neighbourhood, one verified-history comparator
under churn, and searches with 21 of 72 cells flagged. Their top recommendation: a churn validation fixed before the
outcomes, on newly drawn markets, with the runner-up comparator and stronger search checks. The author chose on
2026-11-16 (card `touchstone-churn-replication`): "Plan it now (no spend)". Nothing runs before internal consensus and a
separate spend card.

**Changes from draft 1** (internal review round 1, `docs/revision/roster/{sol,astra}-replication-plan-r1.md`, both reject):
1. Blocking: continuation under churn needs a churn-aware adapter (section 4); `convergence_v2.run_cell` imports
   `sim.reference.select_reference` directly and would reuse no-churn candidate scores.
2. The stamped commitment is a manifest that hashes this plan, the analysis code, its tests, the drivers, the parameter
   files and the dependency lock (section 5); one stamp, one draw, no restamping or redraw.
3. Winner rules for unflagged, flagged, unresolved, cost-stopped and missing cells; round 1 of continuation repeats the
   doubling (section 4). Normalised gain defined; attack and reference terms reported (section 2).
4. The support criterion is labelled descriptive and gets a material companion; expectation 4 states a prospective
   probability instead of "risky"; expectation 5's denominator is defined (section 3).
5. Gates: ordinary p = 0 against churn-path p = 0, and continuation round 1 against this run's doubling record; memory,
   worker limits, atomic saves and resume tests (section 5).
6. Cost from a stage schedule with memory-safe workers, gates, calibration, diagnostics and a flag-share sensitivity
   (section 6). 7. Interpretation without causal attribution and restricted to the generator's neighbourhood (section 8).

**Changes from draft 2** (internal review round 2, `docs/revision/roster/{sol,astra}-replication-plan-r2.md`, both reject):
1. The stamped manifest's bytes, not this plan, are the `--protocol` input of the block reader and of the instance
   wrapper, which then checks every listed hash; the manifest also binds the frozen code revision and the imported
   helpers (section 5). Private stamp and public commitment are distinguished; without publication before H the draw is
   recorded as nonconfirmatory.
2. The continuation gate runs at nonzero churn and tests candidate replay (section 5).
3. Failure rules: a round-1 mismatch stops all continuation, with no substitution; a missing continuation output falls back to the
   validated frozen winner; a missing base evaluation or doubling makes the cell unscorable; the inherited tie rule;
   reselected references affect only diagnostics and stopping (section 4).
4. Interval construction and coverage stated; replication wording tied to the criterion that holds; "not scorable"
   distinguished from "not met" (sections 2, 3, 8). Expectation 4: "no direct runner-up churn result", 0.5 subjective.
5. Cost schedule in execution order (D2+H3 menus before X7-SC, gates after their inputs), churn slowdown on diagnostics,
   preparation and transfer for each of two pods (section 6). 6. Deterministic continuation priority under a cost stop,
   pod-memory admission against the allocated pod, validated configuration reuse across pods (section 5).

**Review round 3** (`docs/revision/roster/{sol,astra}-replication-plan-r3.md`): Sol and Astra accept-with-caveat; the
caveats are applied below without another round: the gates are split and ordered (section 5); the frozen code is
verified at run time and publication evidence is kept (section 5); a continuation stopped between rounds keeps the
frozen winner (section 4); expectation 4 is an uncertain forecast (section 3).

**Status.** Draft 3, consensus. The implementation (drivers, adapter, analysis, tests) is reviewed separately before
the stamp. A prospective follow-up: this plan, its expectations and its analysis code are committed and stamped
before the block that draws the markets exists. It is still outside the registered protocol v2 and changes no registered
decision. It is the first churn result in this project whose markets were unknown when its expectations were written.

## 1. Design

- **Markets.** Six new markets from the protocol-v2 generator (`sim/instances.py`), seeded by a new block: this plan file
  is listed in a manifest (section 5) that is stamped with OpenTimestamps; A is the proof's lowest valid attestation;
  the draw block is H = A + 144, read with `scripts/block_draw.py --protocol <the manifest>`; a wrapper under `docs/` calls `draw_v2.instances(..., calibrate=False)` (the CLI calibrates by
  default) to derive the instances, the 200 evaluation seeds per market and the identity salt from the block root; the
  wrapper installs the draw-derived salt and checks it in every job. The generator labels them `fresh0` to `fresh5`; in this plan and the
  paper they are `rep0` to `rep5`, and every run writes into its own output directory (no record shares a directory
  with the registered `fresh*` records). The headline market is not used.
- **Calibration.** The instances are calibrated once, on the pod's Linux x86 stack, by a wrapper that calls the frozen
  calibration; the configurations and their hashes are saved before any search and passed as `cfg=` to every job. No
  later stage recalibrates (lesson 2026-11-04, the 1-ulp deviation).
- **Rules.** R3 (`F111`), C* (C-mem(6, 20)), the runner-up C-thr(12), and B3 (`F000`), at the registered budget,
  sanctions and parameters (rho = 0.2). C-thr(12) runs under the existing `C*` label with the runner-up parameters in its
  own output directories, as Run A did (a new rule label would fail in the frozen `rule_spec`).
- **Rates.** p = 0, 0.03, 0.05: no churn, and the two rates at which draft 10 found a deterioration on every market under
  both search versions. Mean identity lifetimes 33 and 20 rounds.
- **Attacker family.** D2+H3 only, with the registered search budget and seeds. X7-SC is searched only as part of the
  reference menus (below).
- **References.** Faithful to the registered procedure: each rule's truthful reference is selected from its menu (its
  own D2+H3 and X7-SC winners plus the winners of the nine bank rules in both families, cuts 3) by the frozen
  `_run_reference`; C-thr(12) uses its own searches and a copy of the bank, as Run A did. The frozen candidates are
  replayed under churn (as in the churn arm); the menus are not regenerated under churn.

## 2. Estimands and primary analysis (fixed now)

- Normalised gain g = (attack profit - reference profit) / (S*/N), per seed, averaged over the 200 evaluation seeds, as
  in the registered tests; the attack and reference terms are reported separately.
- d = g(C*) - g(R3), q = g(B3) - g(R3), u = g(C-thr) - g(R3); per market m and rate p.
- The p = 0 cell of each rule is its menu D2+H3 search record, evaluated and doubled; at p > 0 each rule keeps its
  selected no-churn reference candidate, replayed under the same churn.
- **Primary:** the paired change from the same market's no-churn value, Delta_x(m, p) = x(m, p) - x(m, 0), for x in
  {d, q, u}, at p = 0.03 and 0.05. Reported per market and as the equal-weight mean over the six markets with a market
  t interval (5 degrees of freedom) and the count of markets with Delta < 0.
- **Primary winner set:** per cell, the frozen winner if its doubling check is not flagged, and the final winner of the
  continued search (section 4) if it is; the all-frozen set is reported beside it as a sensitivity analysis.
- **Intervals.** Seed-bootstrap: 95 percent paired percentile intervals, 9,999 resamples, seed 0; seeds are resampled
  within each market, with one index vector per market shared by every rule, rate and winner set (the churn arm's
  pairing). Market level: 95 percent t intervals over the six market values (5 degrees of freedom), which treat the
  markets as a sample from the generator's neighbourhood. Neither covers search uncertainty.
- **Secondary (descriptive):** the absolute contrasts d, q, u at each rate with seed-bootstrap intervals (9,999
  resamples, seed 0) and market t intervals; R3's audit allocation (the draft-10 statistics, including p = 0); efficiency
  under attack.

## 3. Support criterion and expectations (written before the draw)

**Support criterion (descriptive)** for "churn worsens R3's relative position" against comparator x at rate p: mean
Delta_x(p) < 0 and Delta_x(m, p) < 0 on at least 5 of 6 markets, with the primary winners. It allows arbitrarily small
changes and is not a significance test. **Material companion:** mean Delta_x(p) <= -0.05 (the registered Q1 margin).
Both are reported for both winner sets, beside the market t interval, which treats the six markets as a sample and
omits search uncertainty.

Expectations (each scored "met" or "not met" exactly as written, primary winners; frozen winners reported beside):
1. The support criterion holds for x = d at p = 0.05.
2. The support criterion holds for x = q at p = 0.05.
3. The support criterion holds for x = d and for x = q at p = 0.03.
4. The support criterion holds for x = u (the runner-up) at p = 0.05. Subjective prospective probability, recorded now:
   0.5. There is no direct runner-up churn result; the R3 churn results and the two comparators' mechanics give only
   indirect information. This is an uncertain forecast, not evidence that the prediction is difficult.
5. R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs (audited jobs over served jobs of the attacker's identities, summed over the
   200 seeds of a market) at p = 0.05 is below its value at p = 0 on at least 5 of 6 markets; a market where the attacker
   serves no job at either rate (a zero denominator at either rate) counts as not below.

Incomplete data: a cell without a valid record at the end of the run makes every expectation that needs it "not
scorable", reported as such; no cell is imputed.

A failure of an expectation is reported as such and is not read as evidence for a particular cause.

## 4. Search quality

Every evaluation cell at every rate gets the registered doubling check; the menu X7-SC winners get none (reference
selection uses training scores). Each flagged cell then gets continued search with Run B's stopping rule, up to three
rounds in total, where round 1 repeats the doubling (it is checked against this run's doubling record). Under churn the
continuation runs through a churn-aware adapter under `docs/`: it installs churn in every worker and replaces the
direct `sim.reference.select_reference` import of `convergence_v2.run_cell`, so that every menu candidate is replayed
under the cell's churn and no cached no-churn score is used; tests require the replay and the round-1 agreement.
Winners: a cell that passes keeps its final winner; a cell that reaches the round cap keeps its last winner and is
counted as unresolved; a cell whose continuation stops between rounds at the cost cap keeps its validated frozen winner,
its partial rounds are excluded from every result, and it is counted as stopped; a cell not continued because of the
cost cap, or whose continuation output is missing or
nonfinite, keeps its validated frozen winner and is counted as not continued. A round-1 mismatch (round 1 differs from
this run's doubling record) signals an environment or code fault, as in Run B (exit 4): it stops all continuation, no
continued winner of this run enters any result, and the primary analysis is reported as not available; the all-frozen
set is then reported, labelled as such, and no fallback is substituted. A cell whose base evaluation or doubling record is missing or
invalid is unscorable, and so is every expectation that needs it. Ties in reference selection follow the frozen
`select_reference` rule (training score, then its fixed order). A reference reselected during continuation affects only
the diagnostics and the stopping decision; gains always use the rule's selected no-churn reference candidate.
Continuation order, fixed now (it decides which cells a cost stop leaves out): p = 0.05, then 0.03, then 0; within a
rate, markets rep0 to rep5; within a market, R3, C*, C-thr(12), B3. The five continuation workers take cells from one
queue in this order, and a resume continues the same queue from the first cell without a passing record. Passing the
continuation's incremental check is not a claim of convergence or of bounded search error.

## 5. Execution contract

- No change under `sim/` or `scripts/` (code freeze). New drivers live in `docs/revision/followup3/` and call the frozen
  functions directly (`R.market`, `R.job`, `R.stage_search`, `_run_one`, `R._run_reference`, `churn_v2.install` and
  `build_jobs`, `convergence_v2.run_cell`), bypassing `R.main` and the manifest-wide freeze, as the follow-ups did.
- Gates, in order: a Mac smoke run of each wrapper; on the pod, after the menus and references, one p = 0 cell through
  the churn path with churn zero (search, evaluation and doubling) against the ordinary menu search and its evaluation
  and doubling, required bit-identical in all three; then, after the first p = 0.05 cell's search, evaluation and
  doubling (reused in production), its continuation round 1 against its own doubling record, required identical, with a
  test that every menu candidate was replayed under churn (a zero-churn gate cannot expose cached no-churn scores).
- Frozen code at run time: before the draw, before each stage and before any resume, the drivers check that `sim/`,
  `scripts/` and the frozen configuration inputs equal the manifest's git commit (no working-tree change), that no
  environment override is set, and that every manifest-listed file matches its hash. The evidence guards that point at registered cells are replaced by guards on this run's own records.
- Memory: the longest task of each stage measured on the Mac (as the churn arm and Run B did); on each pod, before each
  stage, the worker count is admitted against the pod's cgroup memory limit with 25 percent headroom over the measured
  peak and lowered if needed, then kept across restarts; the saved configurations are reused on the second pod only
  after their hashes match the first pod's record; worker-side atomic saves (Run B's pattern); resume reuses only records with this run's
  binding; failure paths (lost worker, cell error, cost stop) tested before any spend, as in Run B.
- Every record binds to this plan's hash, the draw record, the configuration hashes and the driver hashes.
- **Commitment:** a manifest file lists the git commit of the frozen code and the SHA-256 of this plan,
  `replication_analysis.py`, its tests, the drivers and adapters, every imported helper outside `sim/` and `scripts/`, the
  parameter files and `uv.lock`. The manifest's bytes are the `--protocol` input of `block_draw.py` and of the instance
  wrapper, which refuses to draw unless every listed file matches its hash. The stamp, the upgraded proof, the lowest
  valid attestation A and the draw record are kept. One stamp and one draw at H = A + 144: no restamping, no redraw, no
  replacement of a market after any record exists. A valid anchor at A proves that the manifest existed before H; a
  public, uniquely identified commitment also needs the manifest published before H (a push, which needs the author's
  consent), with its publication height recorded and the evidence for it kept (the pushed commit, the remote's record and a block
explorer read at that time); `draw_v2.instances` only compares the given height with H and does not authenticate it. Without publication before H, the draw wrapper records the draw as
  nonconfirmatory (the `--published-height` rule of `draw_v2.py`), and the paper says so.
- Inventory with hashes; verification of every pulled directory on every end state; pod deletion verified; spend
  recorded from the billing page.

## 6. Cost and controls

`python3 docs/revision/followup3/cost_estimate.py`, a stage schedule in execution order at the memory-safe workers
(search 16, evaluation and doubling 9, continuation 5), with calibration, gates, diagnostics, and preparation and transfer
for each of two pods (implementation review r1: the churn search per rate directory, six waves in all; each directory's
serial memory preflight; the continuation gate round always run; diagnostics of continued R3 winners): flag share 0.30
gives 221 core-hours, 29.9 billed pod-hours; 0.50 gives 238 core-hours, 33.6 pod-hours. Actual billing is per pod-hour
(16.74 and 18.83 USD at 0.56); the conservative estimate is the larger of that and core-hour pricing at 0.108: 23.82 and
25.70 USD, with the 1.25 headroom 29.78 and 32.13. The follow-up budget has 48.36 of 72 USD
left. The run may use two pods (menus and references; then evaluation, churn and continuation) to stay under a day per
pod. The stop cap is set on the spend card; the reservation covers 1.3 times that cap.

## 7. Steps

1. Internal review (Sol, Astra) to consensus on this plan.
2. Write `replication_analysis.py`, the drivers and the churn-aware continuation adapter, with tests on synthetic records
   (including the replay and failure-path tests); review; build the manifest.
3. Stamp the manifest with OpenTimestamps; wait for H = A + 144 and six confirmations; draw the markets once.
4. Spend card to the author with the stop cap; reserve in the ledger.
5. Pod: calibration, menus, references, p = 0 and churn searches, evaluation, doubling, continued search, diagnostics.
6. Analysis on the Mac with the committed code; a paper section and table, labelled prospective-exploratory.

## 8. What the outcome would change in the paper

- Expectations 1 to 3 met: the deterioration replicates on new markets under the descriptive criterion; "material" is
  claimed only where the companion also holds.
- Expectation 4 met: the deterioration is not specific to C*; the comparator caveat narrows.
- Expectation 4 not met: "runner-up deterioration not supported", reported without a causal reading.
- Expectations 1 or 2 not met: the draft-10 churn result does not replicate on these markets; the paper reports that as
  its main caveat. An expectation that is not scorable is reported as "not scorable", never as met or not met.
- Every claim is restricted to the generator's market neighbourhood, the four rules, the D2+H3 family and the prescribed
  finite searches.
# Churn replication: implementation and pod launcher (notes for review; no spend)

Scope of this review: the replication's code under `docs/revision/followup3/` against the consensus plan
(`replication-plan.md`, draft 3 with the r3 caveats, commit 3bcd6b1; the cost paragraph of section 6 updated in
implementation review r1), before the manifest is built and stamped. Round 1's corrections and their tests:
`rep-impl-r1-response.md`.

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `replication_analysis.py` (+ tests) | paired changes over the six named markets, the support criterion, the five expectations, absolute contrasts, attack and reference terms, efficiency, seed-bootstrap intervals with one index array per market |
| `commitment.py` (+ tests) | manifest bytes, the required file set and the import closure, hash verification, frozen-code, frozen-input and environment-override checks |
| `continuation.py` (+ tests) | the churn-aware adapter: churn in every worker, every menu candidate replayed under the cell's churn |
| `rep_v2.py` (+ tests) | the driver: instances, calibrate, menu, cthr, gate0, rates (p0 / churn), continue, inventory, analyse; the output binding |
| `rep_collect.py` (+ tests) | the continuation gate and queue, the continued-winner diagnostics, the record loader with validation |
| `rep_pod.sh` (+ `test_rep_pod_harness.sh`) | the two-pod launcher, generated from the reviewed Run B launcher `followup2_pod.sh` |
| `cost_estimate.py` | the committed cost schedule (step estimates below come from it) |
| `test_notes.py` | every ruling below quotes the plan sentence it rests on |

Run the checks: `uv run --frozen pytest -q docs/revision/followup3/` and `bash docs/revision/followup3/test_rep_pod_harness.sh`
(about an hour; the reviewers' read-only sandbox cannot run it, so its log is named in the brief).

## Smoke evidence (Mac; scratchpad `repsmoke`)

Round 1 (2026-11-19, `run2`): the full chain ran in smoke mode on the registered draw (instances reproduce the registered
ones; calibration matches the registered Mac calibration; menus, references, C-thr, every rate, the churn-zero gate
bit-identical, continuation with round 1 equal to the doubling record at differences 0.0, the analysis). Round 2
(`run3`, after the record binding changed the provenance): every stage, instances to inventory, ran (`chain3.sh`): the continuation gate ran with no flagged cell and its round 1 matched the doubling record at differences 0.0; every evaluation and reference provenance carries the manifest and draw hashes (spawned workers included); the analysis scored all 72 cells (0 unscorable, 0 problems); the root inventory verifies (1355 records, 0 bad, 0 not inventoried). No smoke cell was flagged, so the continued-winner diagnostics were run on a copy (`diagprobe.py`) with the gate's continued record: with the frozen winner's knobs they reproduce the frozen diagnostics exactly (audit 23 of 1325 jobs, identical per-seed profits).
Round 3 (`run4`, commit 0bdc0cb, manifest built at that commit; `chain4.sh`): every stage, instances to inventory, ran
(CHAIN_OK); each of the six rate directories has `diagnostics_binding.json` in its inventory; the analysis scored all 72
cells (0 unscorable, 0 problems, gate_ok true, no mismatch); expectation 5 is scored in both sets, so every frozen R3
audit count passed the binding and completeness checks (6 markets at p = 0 and 0.05); the root inventory verifies with
the launcher's verifier (1361 records, 0 bad, 0 not inventoried; 6 more than round 2, the binding files).

## Memory (plan section 5)

Mac peaks, one task in a child process (`ru_maxrss`): a target's reference 254 MB (16.7 s); a full X7-SC menu search
232 MB (5669 s; fresh0, R3, registered market; scratchpad `memsearch`); a full D2+H3 search 183 MB (operations ledger);
one Run B X7-SC continuation cell over three rounds 3.54 GB (`results/pods/memprobe3`). The launcher uses 254 MB for
searches and refuses before any marker unless 16 search workers and 5 continuation workers fit the 28 GB pod floor at
1.25 x the peak plus 2 GB; on the pod every search stage (menu, C-thr, churn search, gate0) admits its workers against
the cgroup limit and keeps the lowest count for the output (`search_workers.json`); evaluation and doubling use the churn
arm's pod-side preflight, whose counts are kept the same way.

## The two pods

| Pod | Step (launcher admission `--hours`, from `cost_estimate.py` at flag share 0.5) | Driver call |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | instances 0.05; calibrate 0.25; menu 7.60 (4 of 5 search waves of both families + references); cthr 1.95 (1 wave of each family); inventory | `rep_v2.py --stage <s> --out results/v2/followup3-rep --manifest <bundle>/draw/manifest.json --draw <bundle>/draw/draw.json` |
| 2 | rates p0 1.68; gate0 1.20; rates churn 9.05; continue 0.51 (the gate cell's round; every later round is admitted inside); inventory | the same, with pod 1's verified output copied in as `results/v2/followup3-rep` |

End states on pod 2: a churn stage stopped by the driver's cap (`churn arm stopped between stages`, exit 1) is "STOPPED
BEFORE a <step> stage (cap, driver admission)"; the churn-zero gate failing is "GATE FAILED at gate0"; continue exit 3 is
"STOPPED BEFORE a continuation round", 4 "GATE FAILED at continue", 5 restarts once on 3 workers with the driver's own
restart estimate, 6 "RUN FAILED at continue (a cell error)". Every failure or stop exit writes the inventory first.

## Rulings

Each ruling quotes the plan text it applies (`test_notes.py`); anything the plan does not say is marked as an addition.

1. **One run tag, one stop cap, one pod name for both pods.** Plan: "The run may use two pods (menus and references; then
   evaluation, churn and continuation) to stay under a day per pod. The stop cap is set on the spend card; the
   reservation covers 1.3 times that cap." Both pods record spend under `replication-<launch tag>` and run under the pod
   name `ts-rep`, so one launch lock covers the replication and pod 2 is refused while pod 1's spend is unrecorded. Pod 2
   names pod 1's launch tag (`POD1TAG`): that spend row must be in the ledger exactly once, pod 1's output must name the
   same launch (`launch.json`), and the two launch tags differ. Pod 2's PRIOR is pod 1's recorded spend.
2. **PLANCAP 33 USD.** Plan: "with the 1.25 headroom 29.78 and 32.13" (rounded up); the spend card sets the cap at or
   below it; the reservation must cover 1.3 x the cap less PRIOR (42.9 at the full 33).
3. **The driver's own admissions count PRIOR plus this launch's closed attempts at the ceiling** (`--prior-usd`, the
   rule of `guards.paid_cost_stop`). Plan: "the reservation covers 1.3 times that cap". The launcher computes it from the
   fixed attempts file.
4. **Menu and C-thr searches have no in-stage cost check** (the frozen `R._pool` runs a whole family); the launcher's
   step admission and the watchdog bound them. Plan: "the reservation covers 1.3 times that cap". A long stage can pass
   the cap by at most that stage (the author's rule: do not kill jobs), plus the transfer and deletion time after it; the
   watchdog deletes the pod at 1.3 x the cap.
5. **The continuation gate is the first p = 0.05 cell, rep0 R3, whether flagged or not** (r1 ruling withdrawn: it had
   taken the first flagged cell). Plan: "then, after the first p = 0.05 cell's search, evaluation and doubling (reused in
   production), its continuation round 1 against its own doubling record, required identical". The gate runs round 1
   in a child process before any queued cell, also when no cell is flagged; its record (`continuation/gate/`) never
   enters a result. "Identical" means all three of Run B's differences are exactly 0.0, for the gate and for every
   continued cell; a gate without a measured comparison does not pass (smoke runs excepted).
6. **References run serially** (18 targets plus 6 C-thr, 12.7 s each in the benchmark). Plan: "C-thr(12) uses its own
   searches and a copy of the bank, as Run A did".
7. **The calibration runs once, on pod 1.** Plan: "The instances are calibrated once, on the pod's Linux x86 stack".
   Pod 2 reuses it only after the launcher and every driver stage check the configuration hash against the calibration
   record and the record says Linux x86 (the launcher's pod 2 bundle guard checks both the system and the machine, review
   r2); no stage overwrites an existing calibration or `instances.json`.
8. **Expectation 5 uses the primary winners** (r1 ruling withdrawn: it had used the frozen winners). Plan: "primary
   winners; frozen winners reported beside". The continue stage reruns the churn arm's diagnostics for every R3 menu cell
   at p = 0 and 0.05 whose continued winner enters the primary set; a missing one makes that market's expectation 5
   "not scorable" for the primary set. Every diagnostics row is checked before its counts are used or reused (the
   winner's knobs, the ordered evaluation seeds, finite profits, one episode per evaluation seed with the four age bins
   of `churn_v2.AGE_EDGES`; for continued rows also stage, id and provenance; review r2, r3). A frozen row also needs
   `diagnostics_binding.json`, written by the rate stage after the diagnostics step and before the inventory: it names
   the rate, the hash of `diagnostics.json`, and per cell the job id, its provenance and the hashes of the search and
   doubling records the diagnostics reran (review r3). The primary set's count comes from the primary winner's own
   diagnostics: a continued winner's from its continued diagnostics, independent of the frozen row; a frozen winner's
   from the frozen row (review r4).
9. **Publication height.** `PUBH` is required: a block height, or `none`. Plan: "Without publication before H, the draw
   wrapper records the draw as nonconfirmatory". The push of the manifest commit needs the author's consent first.
10. **The record binding.** Plan: "Every record binds to this plan's hash, the draw record, the configuration hashes and
    the driver hashes." Every provenance built by the driver carries the manifest's hash (which covers the plan, the
    drivers, the analysis and the parameters) and the draw record's hash, beside the draw root, seeds, salt and
    configuration hash it already had; every stage checks `binding.json` against its `--manifest` and `--draw`. Before
    scoring, every evaluation and doubling record is checked for stage, id, provenance and values, its exact dependency
    set and every dependency hash, positive normalizers, and the doubling flag against the frozen rule; every
    continuation and gate record for stage, id, provenance, a known status, its own identical comparison with a finite
    round-1 winner, rounds that agree with the status (Run B's pass rule) and a held-out winner equal to the last
    round's, on the job's own evaluation seeds (the gate included); a measured round 1 must also agree with the cell's
    own doubling record and frozen search (held-out knobs and mean, both changes, round 1's best knobs, the doubling
    winner's held-out gains) and carry Run B's empty problems list; a well-formed round 1 that disagrees is a round-1
    mismatch (all continuation stops, no primary set), checked for every cell before any base-record exclusion; a
    malformed round 1 or comparison is an invalid record. The normalizer checks run in the shared validation (gate,
    continuation, resume, evaluation and doubling records). On resume, a saved record whose well-formed provenance (the
    job's own fields, each of the same JSON type and not empty) names another job or run refuses the run; a missing or
    malformed provenance makes it an invalid record. A mismatch needs measured evidence: Run B's mismatch status counts
    only with its well-formed failing comparison, and both doubling changes must be finite (review r6, r7). A wrong value type anywhere,
    a record root that is not an object, a number too large for a float, or a normalizer that is not positive after
    the division by N is a failed check, not an error; identity comparisons use strict JSON equality (review r4, r5). The same check runs before a saved record is reused
    on resume; a record that fails it, an unknown status included, is set aside and its cell rerun (review r4). In the analysis, a flagged cell whose
    continuation record fails it keeps its validated frozen winner and counts as not continued, as section 4 says for
    missing or nonfinite output (review r2, r3).
11. **Kept worker counts.** Plan: "lowered if needed, then kept across restarts". Search workers: `search_workers.json`;
    continuation: `continuation_workers.json`; continued diagnostics: `diagnostics_workers.json` (review r2), requested
    from `--workers-cont`, which the launcher lowers on its restart after a lost worker, so a diagnostics loss also
    restarts on fewer workers (review r3); evaluation and doubling: the churn arm's saved counts.
12. **What the manifest cannot list** (partial decline of Astra r1 item 1). Plan: "every imported helper outside `sim/`
    and `scripts/`". The Mac-side launcher helpers (`results/pods/podctl.py`, `runpod.py`, `case_end_check.py`) are not
    in the repository and are not imported by the drivers; the launcher checks its own bytes against the manifest's
    `rep_pod.sh`, and the pod-side code guard checks every listed file in the tarball. Caveat (Astra r2): the launcher's
    self-check does not verify these Mac helpers, so their correctness rests on their own harnesses
    (`test_rep_pod_harness.sh` runs the real `podctl.py` and `case_end_check.py`), not on the manifest.

## Pre-launch steps (after review consensus; none done yet)

1. Commit every correction; build the manifest (`commitment.py build`, which refuses a list without the required set
   and the drivers' import closure).
2. Author's consent, then push the manifest commit (no `sealed/` files); record the publication height.
3. Stamp the manifest (OpenTimestamps); wait for H = A + 144 and six confirmations; draw once.
4. Spend card with the stop cap; reserve `replication-<pod 1 tag>` in the ledger (1.3 x cap).
5. Code tarball from a clean clone at the launch commit (`tar --uid 0 --gid 0`, `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1`); bundle 1 =
   `draw/{manifest.json, manifest.json.ots, draw.json}`.
6. `DRYRUN=1` guard check; launch pod 1 detached with a pid file.
7. After pod 1: record its spend; bundle 2 = bundle 1 + `rep/` (pod 1's verified output); reserve
   `replication-<pod 2 tag>`; dry run with `POD1TAG`; pod 2.
# Lessons, revision program (protocol v2)

Read this before any operations step. One entry per mistake: what happened, the guard that now prevents it, and the guard's kind (check > written rule > memory note). A repeated mistake means its guard failed: replace it with a stronger kind.

| Date | Mistake | Guard | Kind |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-10-28 | The pod tarball carried the laptop user's uid; git on the pod refused the repository ("dubious ownership") and all three tuning pods failed their prep check. | Build every pod tarball with `tar --uid 0 --gid 0`. Since 2026-11-02 every launcher refuses a tarball with a non-root member before creating a pod (tarball guard, below). | check (launcher) |
| 2026-10-28 | The launcher's seed push targets a remote directory that does not exist before the run; `comparator.json` never reached the C*-t pod and the run failed at start. | Put every input a pod run needs inside the tarball (under a git-ignored path if it is not tracked); do not use the seed argument. | rule |
| 2026-10-28 | A zsh loop used `set -- $t` to split a string; zsh does not word-split, so every budget reservation failed on its arguments. | Write budget and launch commands out explicitly, one per line; do not split strings into arguments in zsh. | rule |
| 2026-11-02 | The approval commit added a `.gitignore` line; `.gitignore` is outside the data-only paths, so every later commit failed `promote_v2.same_code` and coverage refused the checkout. | Restored `.gitignore` (91c80ff); the lock file is excluded through `.git/info/exclude`. A pre-commit hook refuses, on `claude/vigorous-villani-bbac79` and on any detached HEAD after `h4-base`, a staged path outside `experiments/v2/` and `docs/` (negative tests on the branch and on a detached HEAD: refused). | check (hook) |
| 2026-11-02 | The worktree's HEAD was detached after the ledger commit (unlabelled checkout in the reflog); a commit landed off the branch, and the first version of the hook skipped detached HEADs. | The hook now applies to detached HEADs after `h4-base`; check `git status -sb` before committing on this branch. | check (hook) + rule |
| 2026-10-28 | The H4 session command wrote only a mixed log; when the machine restarted, the session died and the final message had no file of its own. | The resume command uses `-o ~/h4-final.txt`; read only that file. | rule |
| 2026-11-02 | macOS `tar` wrote 709 hidden AppleDouble members (`._*`, not shown by its own listing); on the Linux pod they became fake `._*.json` tuning logs and both coverage pods failed at once (about 0.03 USD each). Tuning was unaffected (its code scans no folders). | `bench_pod.sh`, `tune_pod.sh`, `cov_pod.sh` refuse, before creating a pod, a tarball with any `._` member or non-root owner (Python `tarfile` count); negative test on the bad tarball: refused. Build with `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1 tar --no-mac-metadata --uid 0 --gid 0`. | check (launcher) |
| 2026-11-02 | The frozen `coverage_v2.build_market_pool` gives each worker one slice of all 20,000 seeds and `run_focal` keeps every episode record of a slice in memory; at full size the pods ran out of memory (`BrokenProcessPool`) on both markets. The 20-seed smoke could not show it; the benchmark priced coverage from per-episode time without a full-size run. | Before the first paid run of any producer, run it locally on at least 1 percent of its full size and check peak memory per worker (`/usr/bin/time -l`) scaled to the full size against the pod's RAM. Workaround for the frozen code: the orchestration driver `results/pods/cov_driver.py` calls the frozen `build_pool` on 25-seed slices; its pool file is byte-identical to the frozen builder's on a 40-seed pool. Code fix (bounded memory in `build_market_pool`) deferred to after the program: a code change now would invalidate the promoted tuning. | rule + equivalence check |
| 2026-11-02 | The coverage tarball was a shallow clone (`--depth 1`) of a data-only descendant; on the pod `same_code` could not prove the ancestry of 627a2de, so the frozen builder refused the finished headline pool after 46 minutes (about 0.45 USD lost). Earlier pods ran the code commit itself, which needs no history. | Launchers require `CODE_COMMIT` and refuse, before creating a pod, a tarball whose unpacked HEAD is not that commit or a provable data-only descendant (history guard; negative test on the shallow tarball: refused). Build tarballs from full clones. The driver checks `same_code` before any episode. | check (launcher) |
| 2026-11-02 | I edited `cov_pod.sh` while a launch was running it; bash reads a script as it executes, the running launch hit a syntax error after creating its pod, and its cleanup deleted the pod (seconds, no run). | Every launcher copies itself to `snap_<name>_<pid>.sh` at start and runs the copy (snapshot guard; test: the launch ran from its snapshot). | check (launcher) |
| 2026-11-02 | I gave the author the signing rebase "to run in the worktree" without a directory in the command; it ran in the main checkout and fast-forwarded local `main` from 627a2de to `h4-base` (c5227a0). No commit was lost; the worktree branch stayed unsigned. | Every command given to the author names its directory (`cd <path> &&` or `git -C <path>`); the signing rebase names the branch (`git rebase ... h4-base <branch>`), so it refuses or acts on that branch only. | rule (author commands) |
| 2026-11-02 | Repeat of the author-command mistake: I gave a bare `ots`, which is not installed; the runbook form is `uvx --from opentimestamps-client ots` (`docs/lock-runbook.md`). The author's stamp and upgrade failed; the unsigned branch was pushed before signing. The written rule above was the wrong kind of guard. | Stronger guard: the author's Operation 5 steps are one script, `docs/revision/operation5.sh sign|stamp|push|upgrade|tag`, which sets its own directory and branch, uses the runbook's `ots` form, and refuses each step whose precondition fails (negative tests on unsigned commits: stamp and push refused; upgrade without a proof and tag without an attestation refused). Future author steps get such a script, not loose commands. | check (script) |
| 2026-11-03 | After the tag `protocol-v2` was pushed, the author's Operation 5 script ran `sign` again; it re-signed all 22 commits, including the attested proof commit, and moved the local branch to copies (a91cfa1) that differ from the tagged d5e602e only in signatures. The tag and the remote were unchanged; the local branch was reset to d5e602e. The script checked each step's precondition but not that the operation had finished. | The script refuses every step once the tag exists (negative test: sign, stamp, push, upgrade, tag all refused at d5e602e), and `sign` refuses when nothing is unsigned. Author scripts end with a terminal-state check, not only per-step preconditions. | check (script) |
| 2026-11-03 | The Operation 5 script's `tag` step printed "ok: tagged and pushed" after `git tag` failed ("tag 'protocol-v2' already exists"): under `set -e`, a failure in the left part of `a && b` does not stop the script. Nothing was pushed or changed. | Author scripts put each command that must succeed on its own line; `&&` only for intended conditionals (the tag guard). Demonstrated: `false && true` continues, `false` alone exits 1. | rule (author scripts) + demonstration |
| 2026-11-03 | Repeat of the zsh word-splitting mistake: an inline launcher test passed `env $common` in zsh, which gave `env` one argument, so no guard ran. The earlier written rule did not hold. | Stronger guard: launcher tests are bash script files (`results/pods/test_eval_guards.sh`, run with `bash`), never inline zsh; the test fails unless each case prints its expected line. | check (test script) |
